  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows a prefrontal trough crossing \nnorthern Wisconsin ahead of a cold front positioned over northern \nMinnesota. Upper-air analysis displays broad mid-level troughing \nover Ontario and Quebec, while an expansive surface high pressure \nridge builds across the Northern Plains. The cold front will pass\nthrough dry tonight, shifting surface winds to the west by \nWednesday morning.\n\nGusty Winds And Fire Weather Wednesday: Deep boundary layer \nmixing up to 750-700mb behind the cold front will drive minimum \nrelative humidity values down to 27 to 35 percent Wednesday \nafternoon across Marinette, Florence, and Forest counties, where\nthere is a moderate fire danger. Modestly tight surface pressure \ngradients will combine with momentum transfer aloft to yield gusty\nwest winds in these areas. NBM probabilistic guidance indicates a\n60 to 80% chance of wind gusts exceeding 30 mph across the\nnorthwoods. Relative humidity values will rapidly recover above \n85 percent Wednesday evening as surface winds subside beneath \nbuilding high pressure.\n\nThursday Night Patchy Frost Potential: Canadian surface high \npressure building over the western Great Lakes Thursday night will\ncreate favorable radiational cooling conditions beneath \ndecoupling winds and clear skies. Overnight minimum temperatures \nwill drop into the mid to upper 30s across north-central and far \nnortheast Wisconsin, including Rhinelander, Land O Lakes, and \nCrandon. NBM temperature percentiles show a 30 to 50 percent \nprobability of low temperatures falling to 37 degrees or colder \nin sandy soil areas, supporting patchy frost formation late \nThursday night. Rapid diurnal warming after sunrise Friday will \nquickly burn off any patchy frost with daytime highs recovering \ninto the upper 60s.\n\nWeekend Unseasonable Warmth And Strong Winds: Strong warm \nadvection and return southerly flow will surge across Wisconsin \nFriday night into the weekend as high pressure shifts to the East \nCoast. Inland maximum temperatures Saturday and Sunday will likely reach\nthe upper 70s to lower 80s, approaching daily record highs at some\nlocations. Tightening regional pressure gradients will produce \nstrong southerly wind gusts on Saturday, with NBM guidance showing\na 30 to 50% probability of gusts exceeding 35 mph area- wide. \nHigh confidence exists in these unseasonably warm temperatures as \nNBM 25th to 75th percentile spreads remain narrow at 2 to 4 \ndegrees F across most of the area.\n\nAn advancing upper- level trough and associated surface cold \nfront will cross Wisconsin Sunday night into Monday, bringing \nreturning rain shower chances. Limited moisture transport ahead of\nthe boundary will restrict precipitation coverage, keeping \n24-hour rainfall probabilities between 30 and 50 percent across \nnorth-central and northeast Wisconsin. NBM probabilistic guidance \nindicates a 15-30 percent probability of 24-hour rainfall \nexceeding 0.50 inches, with expected liquid accumulations \nremaining around 0.25 inches. Post-frontal cold advection Monday \nnight into Tuesday will drop daytime highs back into the upper 50s\nand lower 60s for early next week.",
